{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966802,"nonce":"AgAce1YsmDHQWmEI4/Z0qV62v6U=","title":"Special Unicode chars (⌘, ⇧, ⌥, ⌃ — Apple modifier glyphs and similar symbol-font","message":"**Symptom.** A keyboard-shortcut chip rendered as e.g. `⌘ + D` shows the `⌘` glyph sitting visibly lower (or higher) than the `+` and `D`. Reported by the user from the node_editor_demo's context-menu shortcut display.\n\n**Why (hypothesis).** Most Apple modifier glyphs (`⌘ U+2318`, `⇧ U+21E7`, `⌥ U+2325`, `⌃ U+2303`) live outside the typical sans-serif glyph coverage. The text shaper falls back to whatever font in the registered fallback chain covers them (often a symbol or Apple-system font) — those fallbacks ship a different vertical metrics box (ascent / descent / cap height) than the primary font, so the glyph's baseline lands at a different y when composited inline with ASCII text.\n\n`blinc_text`'s shaper likely uses each font's own metrics to position the glyph rather than retargeting fallback runs to the primary font's baseline. Pre-fix every cross-font run in this codebase will inherit the same drift; this is just the most-visible case.\n\n**How to apply.** When investigating:\n\n1. Check `blinc_text`'s shaping pipeline for how fallback-font runs are aligned. Look for a \"use primary font's ascent as the run baseline\" path; if absent, that's the bug.\n2. Workaround at the call-site: replace the bare modifier glyphs with the Tabler / lucide square-pill SVGs (e.g. tabler `command` icon) and render those next to the letter — they're glyphs from the same icon font and stay on-grid.\n3. Cheaper workaround: use ASCII text for shortcuts everywhere (`Cmd+D` / `Ctrl+D` / `Shift+`) regardless of platform, accepting the loss of platform feel.\n\n**Severity:** cosmetic. User explicitly tagged \"not priority.\"\n\n**Repro:** node_editor_demo → right-click a node → look at the \"Duplicate\" row's shortcut chip → `⌘` sits below the `+` and `D` baseline.\n\nRelated: any cn / theme widget that mixes ASCII and symbol-class glyphs in the same text run (toast titles with check / warning unicode markers, menu items with em-dash separators, badge labels with arrows / pipes).","files":null}]}